nodecode-moment is a rewrite of noprijbi99-dotcom's noprijbi99-dotcom/moment:

  https://github.com/noprijbi99-dotcom/moment
  commit 1af58dda54a9ad9310acb4cad928a6db63f8403b
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 noprijbi99-dotcom (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's core promise, kept in the same words — the
files a session is built on are fingerprinted at its first turn, re-checked on
every turn after, and when one changed the model is handed a receipt naming the
file, its path, the baseline and detection times, the changed lines, and the
current text, so it answers from the present and says plainly that its memory
drifted. The three-file set follows this harness's house files: SOUL.md, the
soul a channel reads before every message; AGENTS.md, the rules every request
reads; and SYSTEM.md, the prompt a turn opens under. Its moment_check tool
keeps its name and its one optional argument; the pre_llm_call hook becomes
this harness's :round point,
which records the receipt into the request about to be built through
nlk:record-turn-context-prompt, so a running turn hears it without stopping.
The report-once rule, the advancing baseline, the session-start baseline the
report measures against, the caps on stored text and quoted lines, and the
state-file pruning (50 sessions, 30 days) are the original's, reimplemented in
Common Lisp with no dependency. /moment and /moment reset are additions of the
rewrite, the tool being the only way in there.

What was left out, and why: the original watched memories/MEMORY.md and
memories/USER.md, one file each. A memory here is a definition, one file per
memory in a knowledge cell, so there is no single MEMORY.md to fingerprint;
moment.watch takes a folder of definitions instead, and the default set stays
the three house files every session reads. Left out too: the original's
difflib-based unified diff (this rewrite cuts the shared head and tail and
quotes what is left), its per-file 20,000-character diff store (max_chars,
2000, covers the receipt and the store alike), and its silent ordering of
receipts (files are reported in path order).
